The Long Game =C2=B7 Strategy weekly =C2=B7 Issue 74

THE SILICON SHIELD IS A WASTING ASSET

For a decade the comfortable argument about the Taiwan Strait went like this:=
 the world's most advanced chips are made on one island, so no one, Beijing i=
ncluded, can afford a war that stops the fabs. Deterrence by indispensability.

Three things are eroding that argument, slowly and all at once.

First, the fabs are moving, a little. The new plants in Arizona, Kumamoto and=
 Dresden will not match Hsinchu's leading node for years, but they change the=
 question from "no advanced chips at all" to "fewer and more expensive chips =
for a while". That is a cost governments can imagine absorbing.

Second, Beijing has spent five years building a domestic supply at the older =
nodes that cars, appliances and most weapons actually use. Its leverage on ma=
ture chips is growing as its dependence on the leading edge stays.

Third, the shield was always partly a story Washington told itself. Indispens=
ability deters only if the other side values the same thing you do, at the sa=
me moment you need it to.

None of this makes conflict likely next year. It means the margin of safety c=
omes from something other than economics: forces, alliances, and the patience=
 of everyone involved. The question for the next decade is whether those are =
being built as fast as the shield is wearing thin.

Reading this week
- Two long reads on Japan's southwestern island defence buildup
- A data piece on mature-node capacity, 2020 vs 2026

Forward to a friend who still thinks TSMC is a peace plan.

